<!-- ===== SECTION 6: STRATEGIC RECOMMENDATIONS ===== -->
<section id="strategic-recommendations">
  <h2 class="section-title">6. Strategic Recommendations</h2>

  <p>
    Every recommendation below is directly traceable to a finding in sections 2&ndash;5.
    The recommendations are prioritized by impact: addressing the highest-risk, largest-cohort
    roles first yields the greatest ROI for workforce investment.
  </p>

  <div class="rec-grid">
    <div class="rec-card">
      <h3>1. Targeted Reskilling: Excel/SQL &rarr; Python</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Data basis:</strong> 56.5% of SQL-based (Data Analyst) and 43.0% of
        Excel-based (Business Analyst) roles survive to 2035, versus 99.9% for Python-based
        roles (Section 4.2). Python is the highest-leverage bridge skill — it cuts AI risk
        by ~0.35 points and is accessible from Excel/SQL backgrounds. Prioritize Python
        training for Data Analyst and Business Analyst cohorts.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="rec-card">
      <h3>2. Protect &amp; Expand AI/ML Talent Pipelines</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Data basis:</strong> AI Researcher and ML Engineer roles have 100% survival
        and 0.150&ndash;0.253 risk scores throughout the 21-year horizon (Section 5.3).
        Demand for these skills is structural, not cyclical. Invest in university partnerships,
        apprenticeship programs, and internal upskilling to deepen the AI/ML bench.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="rec-card">
      <h3>3. Compensate for the Automation Risk Premium</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Data basis:</strong> High-risk roles earn 36.3% less than Low-risk roles
        (Section 3.3). As AI risk scores continue their linear climb, this gap will widen.
        For roles that cannot be fully automated but face rising risk scores, adjust
        compensation strategy to retain talent — or accept that the market will price them
        downward.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="rec-card">
      <h3>4. Build a Seniority Shield for Mid-Risk Roles</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Data basis:</strong> Senior roles show 94.5% survival versus 83.9% for
        Entry roles, even though risk scores are nearly identical across experience levels
        (Section 2.2). For roles like Software Engineer (survival falling from 100% to
        95.2%), invest in advancing mid-level talent to senior levels — seniority provides
        a measurable buffer against automation displacement.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="rec-card">
      <h3>5. Diversify the Cybersecurity &amp; Cloud Talent Base</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Data basis:</strong> Security (100% survival) and AWS (99.8% survival)
        skills are structurally resilient (Section 4.2). These roles benefit from an
        expanding threat surface and growing infrastructure complexity — both trends that
        AI accelerates rather than replaces. These are the safest non-ML bets for
        large-scale reskilling investment.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="rec-card">
      <h3>6. Monitor Business Analyst &amp; Data Analyst Cohorts Quarterly</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Data basis:</strong> These two roles represent ~21% of the dataset but
        account for nearly all survival-rate erosion (Section 5.3). Business Analyst
        survival drops to 24.0% by 2035, and Data Analyst to 41.9%. These cohorts need
        active career-pathway planning: either transition to adjacent roles (Data Scientist,
        Product Manager) or accept managed attrition.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="callout warning" style="margin-top: 28px;">
    <strong>Data Limitation Note</strong>
    This dataset is synthetically generated with deterministic risk-score progression
    (+0.05 per role per 5 years). Country risk profiles are identical per role, and skill
    demand scores cluster tightly (76&ndash;83). Real-world workforce planning should
    cross-reference these findings with observed job posting data (e.g., Lightcast, LinkedIn
    Workforce Insights) and country-level automation studies. The directional trends are
    valuable; the point estimates are illustrative.
  </div>
</section>

